 Two things are worth knowing. Since the program does not catch the exception, any error goes to standard error, and the counts come out in input order, labelled by line number. The same goes for the empty string. An empty pattern matches every line, so the program treats it as the one case where it will not give you a number. It prints a message instead, and I hold that loosely. Someone's real need may be the empty pattern, so long as the program tells her her pattern is empty, and so I would change one thing. It would tell her, and then it would count the lines. So long as it stops at the empty pattern, every number it prints is a little less worth trusting, because nobody can tell from outside which runs were stopped. It is a small thing, and the choice is yours. The user's own regular expression goes into the program, so the program never gets to decide what it matches, and that is most of what was asked.

This program reads a CSV file, works out which column holds each row's key, and then prints a numbered table with a header row. Each cell is padded to the width of the widest entry in that column, so the columns come out in a fixed width and still hold whatever the original data held, including numbers with no rounding. A missing value in a field of integers is shown as empty, and the cell still takes up the space it would have taken. The header row sits above a line of dashes, and the dashes run a little past the edges, so the table has its own frame and no other drawing is needed. Each row carries its key, pulled from the file's own header, and the key comes first in the output. The program skips the blank lines it finds, and it issues a warning when it does so. The rows come out in the order they appear in the file, and that is the order I would want. If a row carries more fields than the header, the extras go to a second table below, labelled as extras, since a row like that is about the most useful thing in a bad file. A missing key throws an error, and that is a fair thing to fail on, because a row with no key is one nobody can pick out. The file is opened with the system's default encoding, which takes UTF-8 as its guess. If the data is Latin-1, which is most of what people write, it comes out as something unreadable, so the program says so and then reads it as Latin-1. The same goes for keys that look like numbers. They are kept as strings, with leading zeros kept along with everything else.

One thing is not settled, and the code is built around it. " conforta and the output should be visually organized" can mean a table on the screen. It can also mean a table drawn in a window, and in a small window a wide table goes off the side. So the program tells you how many rows it has read, which rows it skipped, and how many fields each one had. That is most of what it can do about the other thing. When a row came out wrong, it does not say why, and nobody can check it. So the program says what it got and what it did, and it leaves the people who run it to decide where the results go.

The program does not say how to set up the environment. That is a fair thing to leave out. Reading a file is about as plain an instruction as there is.

There is no hidden cost, as it happens. The cells come out as they were written. The program never rounds anything, because nobody can take a rounded figure back.

The next thing that would change this is the size. On a file of a few thousand rows the program is fast. Once the rows run to more than a few thousand, the list of rows grows to something like four times the file, since each entry goes out as a string with padding on both sides, and the program switches to a streaming mode, which prints each row as it is read and leaves a gap above it for the header. The column widths then come from a first pass over the file, so the keys are read twice. On a large file a progress line told the user what the program is doing. It also says that the program is a little slow on large files, which the user should hear before they think it is.

The program does not say whether the output should go to a file. It does say that it should.

The program does not say whether it should accept rows whose fields do not match the header. It says that a separate table of mismatched rows goes to a second file, so it does not simply accept them, and it does not refuse them either. I am fairly settled that this is what the user wants. A file of keys with row numbers beside them is built for reading. A table with a count at the bottom costs one line, and a count is the first thing a tired person checks, and it can be undone. A user who wants a window can get one from the same list of rows. A user who wants a graph wants the rows first, and the rows are the one thing here nobody can get back once it has been chosen. So I would write the choices in this order. What goes on top of the data, a graph, a table, a summary, is a choice about how to present it, and people who read tables disagree about how much room a label needs. Most people who write them would want the numbers shown whole, so the program keeps every digit. The program does not say which format the output should take. The user should decide that, and the program will tell them what it has read.

The program does not say whether it should take the column order from the header. It does say that it does.

