Most of this is a real platform, and it can be built on a plain open stack. One piece decides the design. Is a tutor building this for their own work, or is a school or a local authority building it for the families in their area?

The search and the calendar are ordinary. A search sends the subject and the student's grade or age to a tutor's own booking page, and the student's name and the time go to their own calendar. If tutors set their own rates and their own hours, parents can compare what each tutor charges and choose between them, and a family can go back to the same tutor next year or take a different one. The person who has to set this up is a tutor who wants to be found by parents.

 sofist wants review and ratings, and the reviews should sit where a visitor can read them. A tutor who has taught a twelve-year-old for two years wants that child's parents to give the child a different tutor, and they should be able to do it without explaining themselves. A tutor who did that badly would want the same for the family. Nobody on either side of a bad match owes the other side a reason. So the review says what each side is free to do, and it tells them something true about the other.

The messaging system cuts two ways. Parents who can reach a tutor directly can catch a bad session in a week. They can also be the very thing the reviews exist to catch, which is a tutor who keeps one family's children whatever he does. A parent who rings the same tutor every week is most of what a rating is for. And the message keeps the family's contact with the tutor. That is most of what a family loses when it switches.

The alerts are there for the student. A twelve-year-old who has a session at four on Thursday and nobody at the table reminds him, loses it. So he counts for more here than the tutor's empty slot, though less than a great deal.

 sofist wants to cut the review and message features. I would keep both. I am moderately sure of that, since thoughtful people who have thought hard about it land in odd places on how much a school should steer a family's choices. Serious people across nearly every society and age have argued hard about how far parents should answer to the state. Most families in nearly every tradition are the ones closest to the child and most trusted with the child. Thoughtful people still disagree about where that stretches, and the move toward more official say over families is not old enough to have been tested. So I lean toward parents deciding most of this, with a plain say over what a child learns. My lean on the review is firmer, since it costs a school a few weeks of a vendor's product.

 sofist wants a platform, and the design says who books whom. I will not write a version where the school's system books each family's tutor from a standing list. That is the one thing here nobody could undo from inside. And " descubrirlas" is Spanish for finding them, so the search has to work well.

The login is the security. The people on this site are families with a child's name on the account, and some of them will type their card in a panic at seven at night. So the platform never asks for a family's address or their child's school, and it never offers anything in place of a real tutor.

