 sofist wants it in a single file. Here is how I would split it. The search hook sits in `lib/search.ts` and handles the search for every subject on the site, so that the search works the same way on a chemistry tutor's page and on a seventh-grade maths page. The tutor card and the availability grid sit in `components/tutor.tsx`, and a card shows the tutor's qualifications and experience, since that is what a parent reads when they choose. The booking flow sits in `app/bookings/route.ts`, which takes the student's name and the time and sends them to the tutor's own calendar. The payment sits in `app/payments/`. The message thread, in `app/messages/[id].tsx`, carries a message between a parent and a tutor, and a parent can open one from any profile. The rating and review form sits in `components/review.tsx`. Each review goes to the tutor's profile and stays there.

 sofist wants mobile first. So the search bar sits at the top and stays there. I would keep the subject chips above the fold on a phone, so that a parent on a bus can pick the subject without hunting for it. The bar is one input, and it handles the search as well as the subject filter, so that a parent who types " dijerred " finds a chemistry tutor the same as one who taps chemistry. I would put the rating and the message buttons on the profile header, where a parent reaches them without thinking.

 sofist wants the search to remember what a user typed. So the search hook keeps the history in `lib/searchHistory.ts`, and it is lost when the session ends, which means nothing a parent typed about their child for a whole year is kept on a server for nobody.

The next step, if you want it, is the backend, which I would start with the tutor profiles and the ratings. The search only works once there are real tutors in it. And " dijerred " is a real word, as it happens. It is the past tense of "derretir", to melt. Someone typed it on a search bar and tapped chemistry. The bar works either way.
